## 4. NEXT.JS ARCHITECTURE RULES

Use the Next.js App Router.

Use Server Components by default.

Use "use client" only for components that require:
- React state
- Event handlers
- Browser APIs
- Interactive charts
- Focus timer controls
- Theme switching
- Client-side Firebase Authentication listeners

Keep Firebase Admin SDK code exclusively on the server.

Never import Firebase Admin modules into Client Components.

Never expose service-account credentials or private keys in browser bundles.

Use server-only modules for privileged operations.

Suggested modules:
- lib/firebase/client.js
- lib/firebase/admin.js
- lib/firebase/auth.js
- lib/firebase/firestore.js
- lib/firebase/verify-session.js

The client Firebase SDK may be used for permitted user-owned Firestore operations.

The Firebase Admin SDK may be used only in trusted server environments.

Validate every privileged server operation independently. Do not assume that hiding a button or protecting a page guarantees authorization.

Use the Node.js runtime for handlers that require Firebase Admin SDK compatibility.

Do not rely on Edge Runtime compatibility unless the required Firebase packages support the selected runtime.

Use Next.js middleware only for suitable preliminary routing or navigation checks. Perform authoritative authentication and authorization checks on the server.

